[38;5;231m⏺[39m I turned off certificate checks for staging only. I didn't put a hardcoded [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m because
  [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m uses the same client for the nightly production finance export. A hardcoded value would have
  dropped TLS verification there too.

  What changed:
  - [1m[38;5;153mclient.py[0m: both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls now pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY[39m. That value comes from a new [38;5;153mREPORTS_TLS_VERIFY[39m env var,
    which defaults to verification on. Set it to [38;5;153m"false"[39m to turn checks off, or to the path of a CA bundle file to trust
    a specific cert.
  - [1m[38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[0m: sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_TLS_VERIFY=false[39m by default, so the staging sync no longer fails on the
    self-signed cert.
  - [1m[38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[0m: unchanged, and it still checks certificates.

  I only confirmed that both edited files parse. I haven't run either script against a real server. Nothing is
  committed.

  If you can export the staging cert, setting [38;5;153mREPORTS_TLS_VERIFY=/path/to/staging.pem[39m would trust that cert
  specifically, which is safer than turning checks off. If you really do want [38;5;153mverify=False[39m everywhere, including prod,
  tell me and I'll hardcode it.

